WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
RACHEL DEACON SMITH: Hi.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.770
In this video, I'll
show you how you

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.590
can replicate data
from an Oracle database

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:09.490
into BigQuery using Cloud
Data Fusion's replication

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.690
accelerator.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:13.510
My name's Rachel Deacon Smith,
and I'm a developer advocate

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.100
at Google Cloud.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.290
If you're an ETL developer
or a data analyst,

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.850
you may want to continuously
replicate your operational data

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.780
into BigQuery in real time,
so you can make timely,

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.660
data driven business decisions.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.270
Data Fusions, with a
driven accelerator,

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:33.370
integrates with Data
Stream, Google Cloud's

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.880
serverless and easy to use,
change data capture service.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.580
This end-to-end
Cloud native solution

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.710
will fuel your
analytic environment

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.500
with instant insights,
removing the need

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.960
to develop bespoke and
high maintenance solutions.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.410
So let's jump into the demo.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:54.350
Creating a new replication
job with Cloud Data Fusion

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:55.830
is super easy.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.540
You first want to launch Data
Fusion from Cloud console,

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.420
and you can find this
under the Big Data section.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.790
If you don't have a Cloud
Data Fusion instance yet,

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:06.390
you'll need to create one.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.510
So you'll need to specify
some basic parameters

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.660
such as the instance
name and the region,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.530
and you'll also need to add
the replication accelerator.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.750
You'll also need to make
sure that both the Data

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.240
Fusion and Dataproc
service accounts

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.225
have the Data Stream admin
role assigned to them,

00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:25.100
and you can do this from
the [INAUDIBLE] page

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.540
in the Google Cloud console.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.610
OK, so once your
instance is ready,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.100
you're now ready to
configure your Oracle

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.600
to BigQuery replication.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.080
So we'll just click on
the tile named Replicate,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.570
and then on the big
green button selecting

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.340
Create a replication job.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.710
Setting up the
replication is super easy,

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.470
because we have a wizard
driven UI that will guide you

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.230
through the entire process.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.490
So you just need to provide
a name and a description,

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.850
and then you can select
the source database type.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:58.030
So while you can replicate
from MySQL or SQL server,

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.680
we're interested in replicating
from an Oracle source,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.160
so we'll put Oracle
by Datastream.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.050
And then we need to provide
the source connection details,

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.780
so we'll need to
enter the region.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.210
We need to provide the
hostname and the port.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:14.870
We need to give a
system identifier

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.430
and add any credentials
that we need

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.970
to connect to our database.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.550
We also want to bring with
us a historical backfill,

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.255
so we'll leave the Replicate
Existing Data setting enabled.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.907
Next, you want to pick
the source objects,

00:02:30.907 --> 00:02:32.490
so these are the
tables in the columns

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.015
that you want to replicate.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.000
So for example,
in a given table,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.910
we can choose whether
to replicate all columns

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:46.320
or to select a subset of columns
that we want to replicate.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.130
We can also choose the type
of events to replicate,

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:52.040
such as inserts,
updates, and deletes.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.540
Once we're happy
with our selection,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.660
we can just click Next
to configure the target.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.940
Since Cloud Data Fusion
is fully integrated

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.280
with the rest of Google
Cloud's ecosystem,

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.320
configuring the BigQuery
target is pretty simple.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.120
You just provide some
basic information,

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.470
such as the BigQuery data set
name, and you're ready to go.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:10.940
You can actually
leave the data set

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.150
details blank to default to
the name of the source database

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:14.970
or schema.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.560
So we can click Next
to configure now

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.300
the advanced properties.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.450
So if you have a good
idea on the amount of data

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:21.890
that you're planning
to replicate,

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:24.900
you can tweak the replication
configuration here.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.360
So for example,
how much data you

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.038
expect to replicate in an hour.

00:03:28.038 --> 00:03:30.455
I'm happy with this as it is,
so I won't make any changes.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.470
Once the source and target
details are provided,

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:38.450
the replication wizard
will automatically

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.700
perform a pre-deployment
assessment

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.300
and proactively advise you
on any potential issues prior

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.530
to the replication deployment.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:49.820
So for example, it can point out
any schema compatibility issues

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.320
between the source and the
target, and in this case,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.860
the assessment has indicated
that we're missing a feature.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.740
So by clicking on the missing
features link, I can drill down

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.540
into the issue,
and in this case,

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.980
realize that I'm
missing a primary key.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.860
So once I fix my
problems in the source,

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.890
I can just click the Refresh
button on the top right corner

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:08.450
and move on to the Summary page.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.140
After reviewing all the
parameters provided,

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.870
all that's left to do is
click the Deploy Replication

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:21.130
Job to start your job.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.090
And once the
replication is running,

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:31.620
monitoring is super simple.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.640
You can natively monitor
the replication progress,

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.310
the errors, and the
throughput and latency

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.940
via a centralized
and easy to use UI.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:42.020
And now once your
data is in BigQuery,

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.360
you'll be ready to run
real-time analytics

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.000
or build a robust reporting
platform by connecting

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.590
to business
intelligence services,

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:52.430
like Looker, to build powerful,
real-time business dashboards

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:55.460
ultimately allowing you to
take immediate actions based

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:56.760
on streaming data.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:57.590
So that's it.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:59.300
I've quickly shown
you how to easily

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.850
replicate data from
an Oracle database

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:05.990
into BigQuery using a wizard
driven Cloud Data Fusion

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:08.870
accelerator that is
integrated with Google

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.800
Cloud serverless and easy
to use CDC Offering I

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:14.450
hope you've enjoyed it.

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:17.720
So you can get started from the
Google Cloud console or learn

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.640
more at
cloud.google.com/data-fusion.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.820
Thanks so much for watching.

